/**
 * Hero command-centre screen — the animated dashboard inside the MacBook.
 *
 * Inlined rather than referenced as <img src="...svg"> for two reasons:
 *
 *  1. THEME. An <img> is an isolated document: it cannot see `data-theme` on
 *     the host <html>, so the site's light/dark toggle would not reach it.
 *     Inlined, the CSS custom properties in the markup resolve against the
 *     page and the toggle wins over the OS preference.
 *
 *  2. LCP. This is the hero's largest element. As an inline node it paints
 *     with the document instead of waiting on a second network round-trip,
 *     which removes the "load delay" phase that the preloaded WebP it
 *     replaces was fighting (see the git history on hero-product.tsx).
 *
 * ~18 KB gzipped, no SVG filters, and every animation is CSS on opacity or
 * transform only. `prefers-reduced-motion` freezes it on the completed frame.
 *
 * The markup is generated; edit the generator, not this string. Two
 * redundancies the generator emitted have been removed by hand and should be
 * removed at the source as well:
 *
 *  - `@keyframes scwf-seal` and `.sc-wf .seal` were written once per stage,
 *    six identical copies of the same 157 bytes.
 *  - An `@media (prefers-color-scheme:light)` block duplicated the
 *    `:root[data-theme='light']` rule that immediately follows it. It could
 *    never win: <html> always carries `data-theme` (server-rendered `light`,
 *    then written by the theme script before paint), and the attribute
 *    selector outranks it in both themes.
 *
 * Both copies of this string ship on the homepage — once as DOM, once inside
 * the RSC flight payload — so every byte here is charged twice.
 */
